  geopolitics: "The oil channel still splits into a physical leg and a political leg pointing opposite ways, but this week the physical leg deteriorated in a specific way that changes what the risk is. Eleven tankers were attacked while transiting the Strait of Hormuz in the week to October 4 on International Maritime Organization data, the most since the war began in late February, and five more have been struck since - one hit by multiple projectiles off Qatar, one set on fire near the United Arab Emirates on Friday. Twelve Indian sailors were injured on Monday aboard the UAE-owned On Peace, and 24 have now been killed across 100 commercial ships in the strait, the Gulf and the Gulf of Oman since February. The important escalation is not the count but the target: the Revolutionary Guard said it struck the engine room and propulsion of the MV Sun Shine for using what it called an unauthorised route through the southern section of the strait, claimed a second strike on an LPG tanker on the same basis, and has threatened vessels beyond Hormuz altogether. That southern corridor off the Omani coast, under substantial US naval escort, is precisely what carried exports back to prewar levels in September, so attacking it is an attack on the mitigation rather than on the flow. Crude through the strait is now 8.5 million barrels a day for the week to Wednesday, about 40% below prewar, with wider Middle East exports at 15.3 million and down 10%. Politically the opposite holds: Trump has described productive discussions with Iran and pledged not to attack before the November 3 midterms while keeping the blockade and adding sanctions on Iran's oil-shipping networks, and US forces have struck IRGC-linked tankers in retaliation. The price verdict is the striking part. WTI closed Friday at 91.660, up only 0.19% on the day and 0.60% on the week and still minus 10.56% on the month; Brent closed 104.430, up 0.14% and 2.13% on the week. A market that will not pay for the most intense week of attacks in the war is a market that believes the political cap, and that is why USOIL is RANGE rather than LONG - but it also means the escalation premium is unpriced rather than absent, and NBC reporting that Trump's team is weighing renewed strikes is the gap risk no pledge covers. The FX reads follow. First, Canada: WTI is the barrel Canada prices against and minus 10.56% on the month is why a CAD score of -2 and a labour market down 110K jobs in two months sit together coherently - but the two theses are driven by different things, the oil call being event-driven and the CAD call data-driven, so if WTI breaks this range upward, CAD is the first score to revisit. Second, the energy inflation actually moving central banks is gas, not crude, and it got worse this week: UK gas at 199.64 GBp/thm is up 6.14% on the week and 170.48% on the year, EU gas up 7.35% on the week and 184.98% on the year, German gas up 4.61% on the week. That is the real content of Lombardelli's line that rates may need to rise if energy prices stay elevated, and it is why a November BoE hike is near 83% while the oil complex softens - and why gas is a heavier headwind for GER40 than falling crude is a tailwind. Third, the haven bid is still going to the franc rather than to gold, but less exclusively than last week. Gold rose 1.47% to 4,193.87 and silver 2.75% to 60.802 on Friday, driven by a strong 30-year Treasury auction cooling the long end rather than by war, and both remain in drawdown - gold minus 2.86% on the month against a January record near 5,589, silver minus 14.68% year to date. Meanwhile EUR/CHF fell a third consecutive week to 0.92826 and USD/CHF closed down 0.32%, two days after the SNB pushed back on tightening. Fourth, and the thing to carry into Monday: the event most likely to move this board is not in the Gulf. It is the French National Assembly opening its 2027 budget debate on Tuesday, with La France Insoumise committed to censure regardless of content, the Socialists keeping the threat in writing, and the Rassemblement National holding the decisive votes - into a market that spent Friday pricing relief.",
